{
  "id": "80745",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Nuclear Bombardment Reactions",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "15jqci5sbvt0c2j",
                "text": "Alpha Particle Bombardment"
              }
            ],
            "id": "fpv3vt9dary8h2d",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Nuclear transmutation"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The process of changing one element or isotope into another through nuclear reactions or radioactive decay. This change can occur naturally, as in radioactive decay series, or be induced artificially in nuclear reactors or particle accelerators."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1823976293",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2091681095",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the conversion of one nuclide into another. It can occur by the radioactive decay of a nucleus or the reaction of a nucleus with another particle. The first man-made nucleus was produced in Ernest Rutherford’s laboratory in 1919 by a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "transmutation reaction"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The bombardment of one type of nuclei with other nuclei or neutrons."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1469855730",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "310400263",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the bombardment of one type of nuclei with other nuclei or with neutrons. Rutherford bombarded nitrogen atoms with high-speed "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " particles from a natural radioactive isotope of radium and observed protons resulting from the reaction:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "r2ycp1jihjvny3c",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ytmqc3nq3nc4il0",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "dahae60x6r79drj",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{}_7^{14}\\mathrm N\\;+\\;{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_8^{17}\\mathrm O\\;+\\;{}_1^1\\mathrm H",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_8^{17}\\mathrm O",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_1^1\\mathrm H",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " nuclei are stable, so no further (nuclear) changes occur."
              }
            ],
            "id": "qoh8waoa1npnunh",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Another such reaction of historical significance was the bombardment of a stable aluminum isotope with "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1423984583",
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " particles, as seen in the reaction below: "
              }
            ],
            "id": "tftfr49i7g76ttf",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "rsebzmbp4d2ek2b",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "t2umrihcnfxad7q",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{}_{13}^{27}\\mathrm{Al}\\;+\\;{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;{}_{15}^{30}\\mathrm P\\;+\\;{}_0^1\\mathrm n\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;(\\mathrm{alpha}\\;\\mathrm{particle}\\;\\mathrm{bombardment})",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "This was one of the reactions first used to prepare radioactive isotopes in the laboratory by Irene Joliot-Curie and her husband, Frédéric Joliot-Curie, in 1934. The P-30 produced is radioactive and emits a positron according to the following reaction:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "hc4hzng15b8gznp",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "p7wlzjhefvxecna",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "rrhg9yvh6jxp1pa",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{}_{15}^{30}\\mathrm P\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;{}_{14}^{30}\\mathrm{Si}\\;+\\;{}_{+1}^0\\mathrm e\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;(\\mathrm{positron}\\;\\mathrm{emission})",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Si-30 produced from this reaction is a stable isotope."
              }
            ],
            "id": "6ee2bb6f73nos2s",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "To reach the kinetic energies necessary to produce transmutation reactions, a device called a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "particle accelerator"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A device that uses electric and magnetic fields to increase the kinetic energy of charged particles, such as nuclei, for use in transmutation reactions and various scientific research applications."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4257644332",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2252519568",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is used. Particle accelerators use magnetic and electric fields to increase the speeds of nuclear particles. In all accelerators, the particles move in a vacuum to avoid collisions with gas molecules. When neutrons are required for transmutation reactions, they are usually obtained from radioactive decay reactions or various nuclear reactions occurring in a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "nuclear reactor"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " A system that harnesses energy from nuclear fission by maintaining a controlled chain reaction. It safely manages the release of energy to produce electricity or heat or for scientific research. "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "822633656",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2869308583",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "j1s2wyx49o567o2",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "jch8y768hyvxlwj",
                "text": "Neutron Bombardment"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4c64p5nj0cqdbjq",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Before 1940, the heaviest known element was uranium, whose atomic number is 92. Today, many artificial elements have been synthesized and isolated, including several on such a large scale that they have had a profound effect on society. One of these—element 93, neptunium (Np)—was first made in 1940 by Edwin McMillan and Philip H. Abelson by bombarding uranium-238 with neutrons. The reaction creates unstable uranium-239, which goes through beta decay to produce neptunium-239. Neptunium-239 is also radioactive and also goes through beta decay to produce plutonium-239. The nuclear reactions are:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4z92zdrouui6bh7",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "8ndovhr5cx26aul",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2837668292",
            "src": "{}_{92}^{238}\\mathrm U\\;+\\;{}_0^1\\mathrm n\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;{}_{92}^{239}\\mathrm U\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;(\\mathrm{neutron}\\;\\mathrm{bombardment})",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "4bzjhya26p1t2gs",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "4251460935",
            "src": "{}_{92}^{239}\\mathrm U\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_{93}^{239}\\mathrm{Np}\\;+\\:{}_{-1}^0\\mathrm e\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;(\\mathrm{beta}\\;\\mathrm{decay})",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "hb5onrei7mgxz2o",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2554374674",
            "src": "{}_{93}^{239}\\mathrm{Np}\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_{94}^{239}\\mathrm{Pu}\\;+\\:{}_{-1}^0\\mathrm e\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;(\\mathrm{beta}\\;\\mathrm{decay})",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "It is possible to summarize these equations as:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "u43epy12hb9mesg",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "gop9f6hjmeggscf",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "frjxk3eq95045sv",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{}_{92}^{238}\\mathrm U\\;+\\;{}_0^1\\mathrm n\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_{92}^{239}\\mathrm U\\;\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm\\beta^-}\\;{}_{93}^{239}\\mathrm{Np}\\;\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm\\beta^-}\\;{}_{94}^{239}\\mathrm{Pu}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "3430865735",
            "type": "p"
          }
